{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Speak to the children of Israel, saying.</b> The word <b>lemor</b> ('saying') — it intends that this commandment is an aspect of elevation and honor for them, to be guarded from impure things. And as they said in the Zohar (Leviticus Rabbah 13:2), Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai said on the verse (Habakkuk 3:6): ״עָמַד וַיְמֹדֶד אֶרֶץ״ — HaShem measured all the nations and found no nation that was worthy, etc. And the meaning of the word ״lemor״ here is in the manner of (Isaiah 3:10) ״אִמְרוּ צַדִּיק״ and so forth. Further, it intends on this path: ״lemor zot״ ('saying: this') and so forth — meaning, that they should take in hand every species of creature and say to them, 'This you may eat and this you may not eat,' and it should not suffice to tell them orally." "<b>This is the creature, etc.</b> The reason it designates the name חַיָּה ('living creature') for בְּהֵמָה ('animal') — I have already written in several places that the aspect of evil is not ascribed to it the aspect of חַיִּים ('life'). And thus His saying <b>this is the creature</b> means that in it there is an aspect called חַיָּה ('living'), to exclude other animals that do not possess holiness, which is the aspect of חַיִּים (life):"], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {0 "He attributes this teaching to the Zohar but cites Leviticus Rabbah 13:2 as the reference; translating the citation as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:55:16.042970668Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}